Micrometer is metrics facade for Spring Boot. Supports multiple backends (Prometheus, Datadog, New Relic). Implementation:

@Service
public class PaymentService {
  private final MeterRegistry registry;
  private final Counter paymentsProcessed;
  private final Timer paymentProcessingTime;
  private final Gauge activePayments;

  public PaymentService(MeterRegistry registry) {
    this.registry = registry;

    // Counter - monotonically increasing
    this.paymentsProcessed = Counter.builder("payments.processed")
      .description("Total payments processed")
      .tag("service", "payment")
      .register(registry);

    // Timer - latency and count
    this.paymentProcessingTime = Timer.builder("payments.processing.time")
      .description("Payment processing time")
      .publishPercentiles(0.5, 0.95, 0.99)
      .register(registry);

    // Gauge - current value
    activePayments = Gauge.builder("payments.active", this, 
        PaymentService::getActivePaymentCount)
      .description("Currently processing payments")
      .register(registry);
  }

  public Payment processPayment(PaymentRequest request) {
    return paymentProcessingTime.record(() -> {
      try {
        Payment payment = processInternal(request);
        paymentsProcessed.increment();
        registry.counter("payments.success",
          "provider", request.getProvider()).increment();
        return payment;
      } catch (PaymentException e) {
        registry.counter("payments.failed",
          "error", e.getErrorCode()).increment();
        throw e;
      }
    });
  }
}

application.yml:
management:
  metrics:
    export:
      prometheus:
        enabled: true
    tags:
      application: ${spring.application.name}
      environment: ${ENV:dev}
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health,prometheus,metrics

Query in Prometheus:
rate(payments_processed_total[5m])  # Payments per second
histogram_quantile(0.99, payments_processing_time_seconds_bucket)  # P99 latency
payments_failed_total / payments_processed_total  # Error rate

Best practices: meaningful names, appropriate tags, choose correct metric type (counter for totals, gauge for snapshots, timer for latency).
